tool_args.old_string:
\paragraph{Diagnostic thresholds.} Based on empirical calibration against performance outcomes across our experimental conditions, we identify three regimes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\hat{r}_k > 1.5$: \textbf{pathological} --- the critic overestimates returns far beyond baseline levels. This threshold reliably separates trained dt\_embedding conditions from untrained/removed conditions (4/4 classification accuracy across seeds). Catastrophic collapse is a stochastic downstream outcome of sustained pathological overestimation.

tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{Diagnostic marker.} We calibrate a diagnostic threshold from our experimental conditions. We emphasize that this threshold serves as an empirical \textit{marker} for identifying pathological overestimation, not as a hypothesis test---it is derived from and evaluated on the same experiments:\footnote{Independent validation on held-out environments (Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup) is in progress.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\hat{r}_k > 1.5$ (at $k \geq 35\text{K}$ steps, where baseline $r_k > 0.24$): \textbf{pathological} --- the critic overestimates returns far beyond baseline levels. This marker separates trained dt\_embedding conditions from untrained/removed conditions (4/4 classification accuracy across seeds 1 and 2). Catastrophic collapse is a stochastic downstream outcome of sustained pathological overestimation. At earlier steps ($k < 35\text{K}$), the baseline's small $r_k$ denominator amplifies noise, producing unreliable ratios.

tool_args.old_string:
\paragraph{Normalized value-evaluation ratio.} Since even the standard DreamerV3 baseline exhibits value overshoot (the critic's predictions exceed evaluation returns during early training), we normalize by the baseline's ratio at the same training step:
\begin{equation}
    \hat{r}_k = \frac{r_k^{\text{exp}}}{r_k^{\text{baseline}}}.
    \label{eq:nve}
\end{equation}

This normalization removes the baseline's own overshoot trend, isolating the \textit{excess} overestimation attributable to the experimental manipulation.

tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{Normalized value-evaluation ratio.} Since even the standard DreamerV3 baseline exhibits value overshoot (the critic's predictions exceed evaluation returns during early training), we normalize by the baseline's ratio at the same training step:
\begin{equation}
    \hat{r}_k = \frac{r_k^{\text{exp}}}{r_k^{\text{baseline}}}.
    \label{eq:nve}
\end{equation}

This normalization removes the baseline's own overshoot trend, isolating the \textit{excess} overestimation attributable to the experimental manipulation. In the current analysis, $r_k^{\text{baseline}}$ is computed from a single baseline seed (seed 1, 500K steps); we will update to the mean of seeds 1 and 2 once the second baseline run completes.
